t1 ZPL features : Data Representation

t2 Presentation

t3 A highly important ZCE

p There are various ways to write down raw values in a Z code : numbers, text, symbols, specific notations... \
As we discussed in the first chapters, one of the main goal of the Z is to be precisely defined from the very beginning \
to the very end to avoid ambiguity or undefined behaviors. \
Therefore, every raw value written in a Z code must respect a certain pattern to be tolerated among the rest of the code.

p In Z, we use the VALUE ZCE to manage raw data given in the code. \
But such ZCE is not only dedicated to this. \
Infact, every time a value is taken from the code to be used somewhere else, a VALUE ZCE is expected. \
That concerns literal data written explicitely by the user as well as the result of function calls or data items content. \
Therefore, those ZCEs are omnipresent in Z, making them highly important to understand. \
If there is one thing you should really be careful while reading this documentation is this chapter.

p In Z, VALUEs are required in basically every ZCI that contains an \uassignment symbol\u \
or inside every parentheses & brackets includers, and even between operators :
" z Figure 18
byt a = VALUE

typ f {
    b = VALUE,
    c = VALUE
}

l = [VALUE, VALUE, VALUE]

m = fun(VALUE)

fun( p[VALUE] + VALUE)
"

p Because there is a huge amount of situations in which they are needed, \
we will not take a look at every case but we will simply explain how a VALUE is read and interpreted by the compiler. \
All the procedures that we will see here are applied at every moment the compiler is expecting to have a VALUE ZCE.



t3 Value Analysis Process (VAP)

p First, remember what we saw in the sum up table of the ZPL before, a VALUE is composed of :
- A type
-
- A value (yes, I wasn't really inspired)
-
p In order to read and correctly interpret a VALUE ZCE, an algorithm called the \uVALUE Analysis Process\u (VAP) is applied on the given content. \
This algorithm is quite complex because there is a large variety of ways to write down values in Z. \
The objective of this operation is to transform the maximum "human-friendly" syntaxes into "machine-friendly" data structures.

l Here is the full \uVALUE Analysis Process\u :
"
.-----------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------.
| Striping blanks |         Remove every BLANK character found at the beginning and at the end.           |
+-----------------+---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                 |                         Detection of an "Operating sequence"                          |
| First analysis  |                          => Operation Decomposition Process                           |
|                 |                                        (ODP)                                          |
+-----------------+-------------------------+-----------+---------------+---------------------------------+
|                 |                         | Includers |  Parentheses  | Explicit priorization (subvalue)|
|                 |                         |           |               |  => Type resulting from new VAP |
|                 |                         |           |               |---------------------------------|
|                 |                         |           |               |  Table shortcut notation        |
|                 |                         |           |               |  => Type tab                    |
|                 |                         |           |---------------+---------------------------------|
|                 |                         |           | Brackets      | List shortcut notation          |
|                 |                         |           |               |  => Type lst                    |
|                 |                         |           |---------------+---------------------------------|
|                 |                         |           | Semi-colon +  | Fixed map shortcut notation     |
|                 |                         |           | Parentheses   |  => Type fmap                   |
|                 |                         |           |---------------+---------------------------------|
|                 |                         |           | Semi-colon +  | Mutable map shortcut notation   |
|                 |                         |           | Brackets      |  => Type mmap                   |
|                 |                         |           |---------------+---------------------------------|
|                 |                         |           |     Braces    | Fly shortcut notation           |
|                 |                         |           |               |  => Type generated at compile   |
|                 |                         |           |               |     time                        |
|                 |                         |-----------+---------------+---------------------------------|
|                 |                         | String => Type str                                          |
|                 |                         |-------------------------------------------------------------|
|                 |   A literal expression  | Character => Type chr                                       |
|                 |         matches         |-------------------------------------------------------------|
|                 |   => It is a LITERAL    | Single-byte notation => Type s1                             |
|                 |                         |-------------------------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | Multi-byte notation => Type ptr                             |
|                 |                         |------------------+------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | Literal integers | Short => Type s2/u2                      |
|                 |                         |                  |------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         |                  | Integer => Type s4/u4                    |
|                 |                         |                  |------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         |                  | Long => Type s8/u8 ONLY FOR 64 ARCH |
|                 |                         |------------------+------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | Literal floats   | Simple float => Type f4                  |
| Second analysis |                         |                  |------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         |                  | Double float => Type f8 ONLY FOR 64 ARCH |
|                 |                         |------------------+------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | Structure definition => Type of the given structure         |
|                 |-------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | VALUE gave the function name                                |
|                 |                         |  => use this function                                       |
|                 |                         |-------------------------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | VALUE gave the name of a DATA ITEM                          |
|                 |                         | refering a function ID                                      |
|                 |   Detecting a non-void  | => use the function targetted by this ID                    |
|                 | returning function call |-------------------------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | In both cases:                                              |
|                 |                         | Function is returning void                                  |
|                 |                         |  => COMPILATION ERROR                                       |
|                 |                         | Else                                                        |
|                 |                         |  => Type is the return type of the function                 |
|                 |-------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | Is a FUNCTION name => Type is fct                           |
|                 |                         |                       Value is function ID                  |
|                 |  Detecting a NAME only  |-------------------------------------------------------------|
|                 |                         | Is a DATA ITEM name => Same type                            |
|                 |                         |                        Same value                           |
'-----------------+-------------------------+-------------------------------------------------------------'
"
b

p Oh I told you it was not a tiny thing but stay calm, we will not see everything now because we will need to see other features before. \
To completely understand the concepts of the the 1st analysis for example, we must explain clearly what are functions, methods, operators and priorities before getting into those mechanisms. \
In this chapter, we will only see the second analysis and even this cannot be fully understood without covering the concept of Z functions.



t2 2nd Analysis

t3 Literals

p It is important to understand that this second analysis is processed \bafter\b the 1st one (that we will cover later). \
Until now, no attribute is set in the VALUE ZCE, but as soon as we get into this second half, a lot of scenarios can happen.

p The first things the compiler will try to find out in this second analysis are \c00dd00\bLITERALs\b\c. \
There are plenty of literal expressions that can be used to write down a value. \
If the VALUE doesn't match any literal expression, the compiler will try its second option : "non-returning function call". \
In the other hand, if a LITERAL is detected, the analysis stops here and the VALUE is set according to the correponding patern.

p \b\cff0000\uWARNING :\u In the following serie of paragraphs, we will talk about some ZCEs that we did not present for the moment. \
I give you this list now for exhaustivity reasons but you better not use the syntaxes of the features we have not seen yet. \
Please do not give that much importance to the specificities of such ZCEs, you will probably get back here when we will define them later.\c\b

l Here are all the \c00dd00LITERALs\c allowed with their corresponding matching patterns :

- Single-byte notation
The character \m`\m (60 in hex) has a specific sens in Z. This is the \braw\b symbol. \
It is used for writing raw data that will be kept until the very end of the compilation \bas is\b. \
Of course, this character is not detected as specific symbol if used inside a text field (literal character or string). \
If a VALUE ZCE starts with this symbol, the compiler is expecting to have a \ubyte notation\u only.

In that case, our LITERAL can be of 2 kinds : a \usingle-byte\u notation or a \umulti-byte\u notation. \
Depending on the character following it, we will consider being in one case or in the other one. \
After a \braw\b symbol, the compiler is expecting to have at least one character among lowercase hexadecimal digits (\m0123456789abcdef\m) or another \braw\b symbol. \
If we have something else, an error will be raised because of invalid literal syntax.

In the case we have an hexadecimal digit, we are in \usingle-byte\u notation. \
The compiler will expect to have a second lowercase hexadecimal digit following it and then, the VALUE is considerated complete. \
The rest of the VALUE, if not empty, will cause an error because of "too much content in literal expression".

The value of that VALUE ZCE is given by the 2 hexadecimal digits and stored on one byte under the type \mbyt\m. \
As you may have guessed, if the character following the \braw\b symbol is another \braw\b symbol, the VALUE will be detected as \umulti-byte\u notation. \
This is the subject of the next LITERAL description.
-
" z Figure 19
byt a = `00 //value   0 in decimal, 0000 0000 in binary
byt b = `f2 //value 242 in decimal, 1111 0010 in binary
"

- Multi-bytes notation
As described in the paragraphs before, a VALUE ZCE is detected as \umulti-byte\u notation as soon as a \braw\b symbol is detected followed by another one. \
After this pair, the compiler will expect to have at a certain number of lowercase hexadecimal digits to read (same set as in \usingle-byte\u notation). \
The number of hexadecimal digits following the \braw\b symbols must be even. \
They work in pair, 2 hexadecimal digits per byte to store.

As soon as another kind of character is detected, the multi-byte sequence stops. \
The same principle as for \usingle-byte\u notation is applied on the rest of the VALUE ZCE : nothing else is allowed because the value is completely set.

The value of that VALUE ZCE will be an identifier given at run time to access to where the given data sequence is stored. \
The type of such a VALUE ZCE is set to \mptr\m.
-
" z Figure 20
ptr a = ``00013eb0a1 //the byte sequence (00, 01, 3e, b0, a1) will be stored somewhere at run time.
                     //the value stored in "a" is refering to this location.
"

- LITERAL integers
This kind of LITERAL is not the simplest one so we will take a look at everything step by step calmly. \
There is a large variety of possibilities to write a literal integer value in Z. \
However, as usual, we will precisely give all the criterias the compiler will apply to detect and read a LITERAL integer VALUE ZCE. \
To make it simple, let's consider that an integer recognition is made in 3 steps: \ibase detection\i, \idigits reading\i and \iterminator reading\i.

For the first step, \ibase recognition\i, the compiler will simply read the first character of the VALUE. \
If it is a \unon-zero decimal\u digit (\m123456789\m), the VALUE ZCE is identified as LITERAL integer \
and we assume that the number is written under the \bdecimal\b base.

If this first character is a negative sign (\m-\m), it is identified as LITERAL integer as well and the number is \
assumed to be written under the \bdecimal\b base as well. Of course, the VALUE value will be stored as negative.

Last but not least, if we have \m'0'\m as first character, the VALUE ZCE is also identified as LITERAL integer \
but we are not able to decide which base to use for the moment. \
To do so, we must read the next character. \
If there is no next character, then this second analysis stops here and the VALUE ZCE is of type \mint\m with value \m0\m (simplest case). \
Otherwise, this next character is used to determine in which base the number is written.

To write under \bbinary\b base, use character \m'b'\m. \
To write under \boctal\b base, use character \m'o'\m. \
To write under \bhexadecimal\b base, use character \m'x'\m. \
Only those bases are allowed for writting integers, every other character will cause an error.

We have now finished with the first step, \ibase recognition\i. \
If the first character of the VALUE is anything else than what we exposed (\m-0123456789\m), \
the VALUE ZCE stays undefined so it will not continue the next 2 steps.

The second step is \idigits reading\i. \
This one is pretty straightforward. \
At this moment, we consider that the VALUE value is set to 0. \
Then, we will simply read all of the VALUE content to get its value. \
Depending on the detected base, we will expect to have only certain characters.

When in binary base, we expect to have only binary digits (\m01\m) starting from the third character (after prefix \m0b\m). \
When in octal base, we expect to have only octal digits (\m01234567\m) starting from the third character (after prefix \m0o\m). \
When in hexadecimal base, we expect to have only octal digits in lowercase format (\m0123456789abcdef\m) starting from the third character (after prefix \m0x\m). \
When in decimal base, if the number is not negative, we expect to have only decimal digits (\m0123456789\m) starting from the first character. \
In that same base, if the number is negative, we expect to have the same thing starting from the second character (after negtaive sign).

There is also a special character allowed when reading digits in any of those bases : the underscore (\m_\m). \
In this context, they are just \i"spacing underscores"\i because they do not have a proper numerical value, \
they are just allowed for better number representation and visibility.

As soon as we finish reading digits (no more character or non-digit found), we go to the third step: \iterminator reading\i. \
The content following the VALUE value of an integer is called the \bterminator\b. \
It serves to determine which type will be given to the VALUE ZCE.

If no terminator is set (no character left), the VALUE is of type \mint\m. \
If it is \m's'\m, the VALUE is of type \mshr\m. \
If it is \m'l'\m, the VALUE is of type \mlng\m. \
If it is \m"u"\m, the VALUE is of type \muint\m (\uunsigned integer\u). \
If it is \m"us"\m, the VALUE is of type \mushr\m (\uunsigned short\u). \
If it is \m"ul"\m, the VALUE is of type \mulng\m (\uunsigned long\u).

If something else is read as terminator, there are 2 possibilities : \
If it is a dot (\m.\m), the VALUE ZCE changes into \uLITERAL float\u. \
Else, an "invalid literal integer terminator" error will be raised.

Just a last thing to consider about terminators: \
If a terminator defines a LITERAL integer with an \uunsigned\u type \band\b the VALUE is detected negative, \
the compiler will raise an error for "unsigned terminator set on negative value".
-
" z Figure 21
//most common cases
int a = 12      //positive decimal
shr b = -35s    //negative decimal (with short terminator)
lng c = 56_000l //using underscores (with long terminator)

//using non-decimal bases
int  d = 0x56
ushr e = 0b0010_0100__0010_0001us //using unsigned short terminator
lng  f = 0o126l
shr  g = 0s

//less common cases in other programming languages
int  h = -
lng  i = -l
int  j = -_
uint k = -_u
int  l = 0x_
ulng m = 0oul

//non-integers: Those examples are NOT LITERAL integers
int n = _    //starting with underscore
int o = _l   //even with a long terminator

//error cases: Those examples are INCORRECT LITERAL integers
int p = 0pl  //invalid base 'p'
int q = 31q  //invalid terminator 'q'
int r = -32u //negative value with unsigned terminator
"

- LITERAL floats
Now that we went through LITERAL integers, we can talk about LITERAL floats easier. \
Those LITERALS are the only way to write down decimal values in Z (that we call "floating point values" for technical reasons). \
We saw earlier that a LITERAL integer can become a LITERAL float as soon as a dot character is found while trying to find out its terminator. \
This is the most common way to write a LITERAL float. \
However, the process does not stop here. Let's go further.

When a LITERAL float is detected from an integer, the value of that ZCE has already been read & interpreted. \
However, such a value could have been written under binary, octal, hexadecimal or decimal base which is \bnot completely allowed\b in LITERAL floats. \
Actually, the only base allowed for writting LITERAL floats is the \udecimal\u one. \
So the first thing you have to know is that LITERAL floats that came from LITERAL integers in non-decimal bases are rejected by the precompiler (an error is raised).

Then, if we are in decimal base, the value previously read from the LITERAL integer is kept but we will continue reading digits as previously done in step 2 because \
we expect to have other digits after the current dot character found (for the decimal part). \
Finally, as for LITERAL integers, as soon as there is no more digits to read (end of VALUE or non-digit detected), we go to the final \iterminator reading\i step \
(specific to LITERAL floats this time).

The \iterminator reading\i step for LITERAL floats is quite similar to the one for LITERAL integers. \
However, please read carefully the following because it might differ a bit from the syntaxes commonly used in programming languages. \
If no terminator is set (no character left), the VALUE is of type \mflt\m. \
If it is \m'd'\m, the value is of type \mdbl\m. \
Else, the same kind of error as for LITERAL integers will be raised because of invalid terminator set.

Until now, each time a VALUE ZCE has been detected associated with a specific LITERAL, \
it is read and interpreted so that its second analysis stops without going further with other kind of LITERAL. \
The second analysis continues only if the current VALUE ZCE is still \uundefined\u.

One last thing to know about that LITERAL is that an \uundefined\u VALUE ZCE can become a LITERAL float if it starts with a dot character. \
In that case, the value stored in the integer part is 0 and the rest of the value read after the dot is making the decimal part of the number. \
In those situations, the process of reading the next digits is the same as for LITERAL floats coming from LITERAL integers \
(which is the same as for LITERAL integers themselves: only decimal digits and underscores allowed).
-
" z Figure 22
//common cases
flt a = 1.3  //integer 1 => float 1.3
dbl b = 2.4d //integer 2 => float 2.4 => double 2.4

//less common cases
flt c = .
dbl d = .d
flt e = 130_250.567_623_654
flt f = -15.

//error cases
flt g = 0x36. //invalid base for writting literal float
flt h = 3..2  //invalid terminator '.'
"

- LITERAL Structure definition
For this one, the syntax looks like some other LITERALs that we will see later so please read carefully this one before going further. \
A VALUE ZCE is detected as LITERAL structure if it starts with a name following by a braces includer only. \
Blanks are allowed between the name and the includer.

As soon as a LITERAL structure is detected, the given name will be analyzed to check if it is a correct structure name. \
If there is no \bdeclared\b structure with this name yet, an error is raised. \
Else, the VALUE ZCE is set of the type given as name and the content between the includers will be analyzed next.

The braces includers defines a zone in which the user can write down which value to set to each of the structure instance fields. \
The content inside the includer will be split by comas and blanks as well as empty lines are ignored at beginning & end of each chunk. \
Each chunk corresponds to a field of the structure instance to be set. \
There are two ways of initializing those fields : using \uexplicit\u naming and using \uimplicit\u naming.

For each chunk inside the includer, the compiler will look for a name followed by an assignment symbol (an equal sign \m'='\m not followed by another one). \
Blanks are allowed between them. \
If so, we consider using the \uexplicit\u naming so the name given before the assignment symbol must be one of the field names of the structure. \
By doing this, you specify which field of the structure is targetted by each chunk. \
Of course, if the name given corresponds to a field already set by another chunk, an error will be raised because of "same field set twice". \
Also, after the assignment symbol of an explicit chunk is expected a VALUE ZCE. This last thing will finally be used to set the targetted field value.

If a chunk is not detected as \uexplicit\u, it is then considered using \uimplicit\u naming. \
In that case, the compiler will automatically detect which field to target for it. \
Following the order in which the structure fields are given in its declaration, the first non-already set field will be taken as target. \
Also, the content of the chunk will be completely interpreted as VALUE ZCE to associate with the targetted field.
-
" z Figure 23
//declare a structure "myStructure"
typ myStructure {
    byt myField1,
    chr myField2,
    chr myField3 = 'j'
}

// ----> Here is the important part
a = myStructure {
    myField1 = `0e, //chunk with explicit naming
    'h'             //chunk with implicit naming (1st non-already set field: myField2)
}                   //default value for myField3 will be set because nothing have been specified here

b = myStructure {
    myField3 = 'e', //chunk with explicit naming (3rd field)
    `ff,            //chunk with implicit naming (1st non-already set field: myField1)
    'y'             //chunk with implicit naming (2nd non-already set field: myField2)
}
"

- LITERAL Characters
As well as we have a notation for writing single bytes in Z, we also have a notation for writing a single character. \
Using the byte/multi-byte notation, you can write text using exact digital values. \
That means you must know exactly which value corresponds to which character in your encoding table.

To solve this, a more textual syntax exists for writing characters. \
Using a \bcharacter\b symbol means that a \bcharacter\b LITERAL is being defined. \
The compiler is expecting to have any \utextual character\u following the symbol and then another \bcharacter\b symbol at the end to mark the end of the LITERAL.

You may have seen I talked about "textual characters" only in those LITERALs. \
Indeed, there is only a certain number of characters allowed: \
lowercase alphabet \mabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz\m, \
uppercase alphabet \mABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ\m, \
numbers \m0123456789\m, \
symbols \m_ .,;:!?-+*/%=&|#$"<>@()[]{}~^`\m and \
escaped characters.

In the principle, we should say that only \bone\b byte character must be given to have a valid LITERAL character. \
However, there are exceptions to this that are \uescaped characters\u. \
You might have noticed that the backslash character is not mentionned in textual symbols. \
This is because using this character is telling the compiler that we are giving an escaped character.

When writing an escaped character, the compiler will expect to have a second character following the backlash symbol. \
Depending on that second character, the LITERAL will take a specific value. \
To illustrate the thing properly, we will give all the allowed escaped characters in the example at the end.

The VALUE ZCE of LITERAL characters are of type \mchr\m and takes the value of the ASCII value of the textual character.
-
" z Figure 24
a = 'z' //value 7a (in hex) will be stored
b = '5' //value 35 (in hex) will be stored

//here are every allowed escaped characters
ALERT           = '\a' //bell symbol            (07 in hex)
BACKSPACE       = '\b' //backspace              (08 in hex)
ESCAPE          = '\e' //escape symbol          (1b in hex)
FORM_FEED       = '\f' //form feed              (0c in hex)
LINE_FEED       = '\n' //line feed              (0a in hex)
CARRIAGE_RETURN = '\r' //carriage return        (0d in hex)
HORIZONTAL_TAB  = '\t' //horizontal tabulation  (09 in hex)
VERTICAL_TAB    = '\v' //vertical tabulation    (0b in hex)
NULL            = '\0' //NULL byte              (00 in hex)
BACKSLASH       = '\\' //regular backslash      (5c in hex)
SIMPLE_QUOTE    = '\'' //single quote character (27 in hex)
DOUBLE_QUOTE    = '\"' //double quote character (22 in hex)
"

- LITERAL Strings
When a VALUE ZCE starts with a double quote character (\m'"'\m), it is detected as LITERAL string. \
This LITERAL is a shortcut for writting a specific structure LITERAL. \
Every character read after the starting double quote will be considerated as piece of data under text format as for LITERAL characters. \
This behaviour stops as soon as another double quote is found.

A LITERAL String will store a \mstr\m structure. \
When the whole string text has been read, the raw data is stored in its \i"data"\i field \
and its \i"length"\i is set to the number of bytes read under \mlng\m format.

The same mechanism as for LITERAL characters is applied for escape characters with the difference that \
Nothing else is allowed after the terminating double quote in the VALUE ZCE. \
The type of such a VALUE is set to \mstr\m.
-
" z Figure 25
a = "blabla"
//is a shortcut for
a = str { length=6l, data=``626c61626c61 }

b = "hey \'you" //OK
c = "hey \"me"  //OK as well
"

- LITERAL Includers
There are 4 types of includers that can be considerated as LITERAL values. \
As for LITERAL Strings, those includers are shortcuts to specific structure LITERALs.

Those structures are \utables\u (\mtab\m), \ulists\u (\mlst\m), \umaps\u (\mmap\m) and \uflies\u (\mfly\m). \
We will not take a look at what are those structures for now because there is a lot of other thing we must see before. \
Moreover, these expressions are interpreted only if the compiler option INTERPRET_COMMON_STRUCTURES is enabled. \
Just remember that those other literal notations also exist and that if we find them at this stage, we will process the VALUE ZCE as one of these common structures.
-

p In the previous chapter, we saw that there were only 7 root types in Z so that only 7 types are defined by default. \
However, in VAP, more types can be given to some value patterns... \
Do we have to declare such types before using their VALUE pattern ? \
The answer is : Yes. \
Infact, the non-root types possibly attributed in VAP are assumed to be very generic types that the user is able to declare himself \
as first requirements before getting into complex data structures and algorithms.

p This is basically the role of a \ustandard library\u. \
In this documentation, the types assumed to be declared in the standard library are at most :
- boo : Booleans
-
- chr : Characters
-
- fct : function pointers
-
- fly : Flies
-
- lst : Lists
-
- map : Maps
-
- tab : Tables
-
- str : Strings
-
p That doesn't mean such types are required to make a working Z code, \
however, to be able to use the full Z syntax with all of its shortcuts notations, such types must be declared before.


t3 Non-void returning function call (!VFC)

p As said before, this is the second option for identifying a VALUE ZCE in the 2nd analysis. \
If the VALUE is a "name" (function name) \udirectly\u followed by a parentheses includer, it is detected as a non-returning function call (or "!VFC"). \
We have not seen exactly what are Z functions for the moment so we will just give a quick explanation of which pattern is expected to match with this option. \
A function "name" basically consist in a set of letters, numbers, underscores and dots. \
As for data items, functions must be declared. \
If such a pattern is detected in a VALUE ZCE, the compiler will look for a declared function with the "name" given and then identify.
" z Figure 26
a = my_function_name(...) //The compiler will look for a declared function "my_function_name".
                          //  Let's say that this function is declared.
                          //  Functions also have an attribute called the "return type".
                          //  Let's say that this one has a return type of "int".
                          // => the VALUE ZCE is of type "int"
                          //    the VALUE value will depend on the function's content (unpredictible)
                          //VALUE is identified => end of the second analysis

b = my_void_function_name(...) //The compiler will look for a declared function "my_function_name".
                               //  Let's say that this function is declared as well and it has a return type of "voi" (void).
                               // => COMPILATION ERROR : "Could not get a value from a VOID returning function call".

c = my_function_name (...) //Note that blanks are NOT allowed between function name and parentheses.
                           //If so, it will NOT be interpreted as a function call.
                           //Here, 2nd analysis continues to the 3rd option : NAME only
"

p When trying to find out a function with the given name, the compiler can behave in 2 ways. \
First, if we are in \uglobal scope\u, the compiler will only have the visibility over \bconstant functions\b (we will see this later). \
Else, every function is visible for targetting.

p In all cases, if a declared function matches the given name but it is returning \mvoi\m, the compiler will raise an error. \
We need a \bnon-void\b returning function call to have a valid VALUE. \
The type of the VALUE ZCE will be the returning type of the function found.

p There is also a case to take into account : if no declared function matches the given name, what happens ? \
In that case, there is still a possibility to find out to which function the given name refers. \
The compiler will look for this name in the declared data items.

p If a data item matches, it will be considered that it holds a function ID. \
However, to prevent hazardous behavior, the compiler will raise an error if the data item found is not of type \mfct[...]\m (which are reserved types for function IDs). \
If a data item has been found with the given name \band\b it is of type \mfct[...]\m, then the function corresponding to this ID will be targetted.
" z Figure 27
//Let's say that function "my_function" exists and its return type is "int".
fct[int] a = my_function //storing ID of that function in a data item

b = a() //calling function "my_function" using data item "a"
        // => VALUE type is "int" because "a" is of type "fct[int]"
"
p It the example above, our data item holding the function ID can be called as a regular function does because the value has been correctly set before. \
However, its is possible that the same kind of data item holds an invalid function ID. \
In that case, we are not able to determine which function to call and also what is the resulting value to get from it.

p For this reason, such executions are not 100% guaranteed at runtime. \
Depending on your compiler options, it can lead to RUNTIME/FATAL errors or, in the worst case, to undetermined executions in your program.



t3 NAME only

p Here we are in the simplest case of VALUE ZCE identification. \
If no LITERAL matches and no function call pattern has been detected, the last option in this 2nd analysis is the "NAME only" attribution.

p This one is very simple. \
The VALUE ZCE must be the exact same name as a function or data item name. \
If it is refering to a \udata item\u, the VALUE ZCE will hold the same type and value. \
If it is refering to a \ufunction\u, the VALUE ZCE will have as \mfct[<targetted function return type>]\m as type and the function ID as value. \
We already used a VALUE like this in the last example (Figure 27).

p That's it for VALUE ZCEs. \
Remember that every VALUE that does not fit with one of the defined cases will be rejected by the compiler because of "Unknown value pattern".




b
